{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfcTNCoGxUMpUMPXjspwH", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "signature": ""}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7816, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16975, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7816}, "output_tokens": 5, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "172f87b7-539c-4abf-80a2-8eedbdd2f5a9", "uuid": "b236a8ea-cbe6-4ba3-90ae-01f37a7a79c6", "timestamp": "2026-10-02T00:21:58.737Z", "request_id": "req_011CfcTNCZPacko8jykEQq59", "thinking_duration_ms": 267}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfcTNCoGxUMpUMPXjspwH", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "**Banker's rounding** (also called round-half-to-even, convergent rounding, or statistician's rounding) changes only one case of ordinary rounding: what to do when a value sits *exactly* halfway between two candidates.\n\n- **Usual school rounding (round-half-up):** a tie always goes up. 2.5 \u2192 3, 3.5 \u2192 4.\n- **Banker's rounding:** a tie goes to whichever neighbor is **even**. 2.5 \u2192 2, 3.5 \u2192 4, 0.125 \u2192 0.12, 0.135 \u2192 0.14.\n\nAnything that isn't an exact tie rounds normally (2.51 \u2192 3, 2.49 \u2192 2).\n\n### Why accountants like it\n\n1. **It removes systematic bias.** With half-up, every tie moves the number upward. Over thousands of transactions, line items, or tax calculations, those half-cents add up to a total that drifts high. With half-even, about half the ties go up and half go down, so the errors largely cancel and the rounded total stays close to the true unrounded total.\n\n   Example: rounding 0.5, 1.5, 2.5, 3.5 (true sum = 8)\n   - Half-up: 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = **10**\n   - Half-even: 0 + 2 + 2 + 4 = **8**\n\n2. **Reconciliation is easier.** When a sum of rounded line items has to match a separately rounded total (an invoice vs. its lines, or a ledger vs. a bank statement), less drift means fewer penny discrepancies to track down.\n\n3. **It's fairer over many transactions.** Neither side (customer or business, taxpayer or authority) gains a consistent edge on rounding.\n\n4. **It's a standard.** It's the default rounding mode in IEEE 754 floating point, and it's the default for Python's `round()` and `decimal` module.\n\n### Caveats\n\n- It only matters when exact ties are common, which they are with currency and fixed decimal places.\n- Some jurisdictions and regulations require half-up for certain calculations, such as some tax rules or invoicing laws. \"Accountants prefer it\" isn't universal, so check the rules that apply.\n- Use exact decimal types, not binary floats. `2.675` stored as a float is really slightly below 2.675, so it won't behave like a true tie.\n\nThis project already applies it: there's a recorded decision with the finance team (March 2026) that tax amounts use `decimal.ROUND_HALF_EVEN`, not `ROUND_HALF_UP`."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7816, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16975, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7816}, "output_tokens": 5, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "172f87b7-539c-4abf-80a2-8eedbdd2f5a9", "uuid": "30f57e97-c6ca-4d89-9513-5b3e860b413a", "timestamp": "2026-10-02T00:22:06.513Z", "request_id": "req_011CfcTNCZPacko8jykEQq59"}
